--- A: To integrate this project with the Spring Framework and provide REST controllers, you can follow these steps:

1. **Add Spring dependencies**: Update the `pom.xml` file (if you're using Maven) or the `build.gradle` file (if you're using Gradle) to include the necessary Spring dependencies. For example, add the following to your `pom.xml` file:
```xml
<dependencies>
    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
        <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-web</artifactId>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
        <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-data-jpa</artifactId>
    </dependency>
</dependencies>
```
2. **Configure Spring Boot**: Create a `application.properties` file to configure Spring Boot. For example:
```properties
spring.datasource.url=jdbc:h2:mem:librarydb
spring.datasource.username=sa
spring.datasource.password=
spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto=update
```
3. **Create REST controllers**: Create a new package, e.g., `com.example.api`, and create a new class, e.g., `LibraryController`. This class will handle REST requests for the library service.
```java
// com.example.api/LibraryController.java
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/library")
public class LibraryController {
    @Autowired
    private LibraryService libraryService;

    @GetMapping
    public List<Book> search(@RequestParam String needle) {
        return libraryService.search(needle);
    }

    @GetMapping("/{isbn}")
    public Book getBook(@PathVariable String isbn) {
        return libraryService.getBook(isbn);
    }
}
```
4. **Configure Spring Data JPA**: Create a `BookRepository` interface that extends `JpaRepository`. This interface will define the data access methods for the `Book` entity.
```java
// com.example.core/BookRepository.java
public interface BookRepository extends JpaRepository<Book, Long> {
    @Query("SELECT b FROM Book b WHERE b.title = :title")
    Book findByTitle(@Param("title") String title);
}
```
5. **Create a main application class**: Create a new class, e.g., `LibraryApplication`, that will serve as the main application class.
```java
// com.example.LibraryApplication.java
@SpringBootApplication
public class LibraryApplication {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(LibraryApplication.class, args);
    }
}
```
